course: dictionary
lesson: 4158
title: Gnaw and gnawing feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Gnaw means to bite or chew something again and again, especially something hard. A dog gnaws a bone, and a mouse gnaws wood. «A rat can squeeze through a hole about the size of a coin, and its front teeth never stop growing, so it gnaws all the time, even through cables.» The clue is that the teeth never stop growing, so the rat must keep wearing them down.
explain: Gnaw also has a second meaning. If a worry or a feeling gnaws at you, it keeps bothering you for a long time. Check it by putting your meaning into the sentence: «Doubt gnawed at her» means doubt kept bothering her, again and again, and would not go away.
explain: The little words after gnaw change the picture. «Gnaw through» means chew a hole all the way across, as in «a little mouse saves a lion by gnawing through a net». «Gnaw on» means chew at the surface, as a dog does with a bone. When you meet gnaw, look at the word that comes after it.
fact: Gnaw means to bite or chew something again and again.
fact: Gnaw is a verb: gnaw, gnaws, gnawed, gnawing.
fact: «Gnaw at someone» and «gnawing hunger» mean a feeling that keeps bothering you and will not go away.
fact: Rodents such as rats, mice, beavers and squirrels gnaw because their front teeth keep growing all their lives.
fact: Beavers gnaw through trees to build dams.
exercise: «The dog began to gnaw on the bone.» What does gnaw mean here? | To run away very fast | To sleep quietly on the rug | *To bite or chew something again and again | To bury something in the garden
exercise: «Doubt gnawed at her all night.» What does gnawed at suggest? | A happy dream | *A worry that kept bothering her for a long time | A bite on her hand | A sudden loud noise in the night
exercise: «Beavers gnaw through trees to build dams.» What does gnaw through mean? | Paint a picture of the tree | Swim under the water quickly | Climb over the top of the tree | *Chew a way all the way through
test: «House mice gnaw holes in packets when they move in for food and warmth.» Why do the mice gnaw the packets? | To grow taller | To hide from the cat | To make music | *To get food and keep warm
test: «Their teeth never stop growing, so they gnaw almost anything.» What keeps a rodent's teeth from getting too long? | Lying in the sun | Sleeping all day | *Constant gnawing wears them down | Eating only soft food
exam: «In Aesop's fable, a little mouse saves a lion by gnawing through a net.» What does gnawing through a net mean? | Tying a knot in it | *Chewing a hole all the way through it | Pulling it up in one go | Sitting on it quietly
